// The blink example, as a package that depends on one — which is what makes this a test of the
// package machinery and not only of the program.
//
// It is built by `sysl build-c` rather than `sysl build`, because the SDK's CMake owns the link.
// That is `pico2`'s doing and is explained in its own manifest; nothing about the dependency itself
// is unusual.

package {
  name    = "sysl-blink"
  version = "0.1.0"
}

// **The current tag, like everything else here.** This program wants nothing the later ones added —
// the LED, `sleep_ms` and `print` were all in 0.0.4 — but a project left on a tag nobody else names
// is one nobody notices has stopped building, and a testbed exists to be what gets tried first.
//
// It is not free. A package's C is compiled whole whatever the program imports, and 0.0.6 put the
// radio's four C files in this one, so `CMakeLists.txt` beside this has to hand `sysl build-c` the
// SDK's include closure and its compile definitions before `dns.c` gets past `pico/cyw43_arch.h`.
// That cost is real and is written down there rather than dodged here.
dependencies {
  pico2 { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/pico2", version = "0.0.8" }
}

// **No `requires` block.** The program asks for no allocator and no operating system: it prints,
// which is one function in the standard module rather than a capability, and everything else is a
// call into the SDK's C.
